        notes: "- Sing your tempo — not just tap it. The accompanist needs to hear the rhythm and feel, not just a beat\n- Tap while you sing — chest, leg, or foot are all fine\n- But the priority in that order is: (1) sing, (2) tap. If you can only do one, sing. Never tap without singing. Tapping alone doesn't give the accompanist style, tone, or feel — it just gives a beat. The singer's voice gives tempo.\n- Tap close to your heart (chest). The heart and lungs are rhythmically connected organs. When you tap away from your centre — on your thigh, your foot, your arm — there's a physiological disconnect that can pull your tempo off. The chest tap keeps the pulse anchored to your core. If you've seen singers tap on their thigh and then speed up or slow down without noticing, the distance from their centre is often the culprit.\n- Don't tap frantically — rapid, anxious tapping signals nerves\n- Don't just quote the BPM number — explain it through singing and feel\n- If you're using a foot tap or thigh tap and it's stable, great. But if you notice yourself drifting, try moving your tap to your chest. It will feel vulnerable at first — it puts your body more on display — but that vulnerability is also presence. You're not hiding. You're communicating.",
