        title: "Key Signatures — The Sharp & Flat Tricks",
        notes: "### Why Key Signatures Matter\n\nYou don't need to be a music theory expert, but knowing a handful of terms means you can communicate with your accompanist. Knowledge is power — a little bit of information empowers you to mark up your music, understand what you're doing, and take control of your performance.\n\n### What Is a Key Signature?\n\nA key signature tells you the tonal centre of the song and which notes are sharpened or flattened throughout. In Western tonal music, most songs attribute to **12 tonal centres** ranging from A to G-sharp. If you count the notes between an A and the A an octave above, you'll wind up with 12 semitones — those are the 12 keys your music could be in.\n\nThat's only in the major tonality, but each major key can be shifted to its relative minor, so technically you can have **24 tonal centres**. But first and foremost, you just need to know what key your song is in — or at least what your key signature shows.\n\n### What Is a Semitone?\n\nA semitone (also called a half-step) is any two piano keys right next to each other. It doesn't matter what colour they are:\n\n- White and black next to each other = semitone\n- White and white next to each other (like E to F, or B to C) = semitone\n\nIf they're next door to each other, that's a semitone. Two semitones equal one **tone** (a whole step).\n\n### The Sharp Trick\n\nIf you're trying to work out what key you're in from the sharps, look at the sharps listed from left to right on your stave. **Take the last sharp and raise it by one semitone** — that gives you the key.\n\n- **G major** has one sharp: F-sharp. Raise F-sharp by a semitone = G. So you're in G major.\n- **D major** has two sharps, the last being C-sharp. Raise C-sharp by a semitone = D. So you're in D major.\n\n### The Flat Trick\n\nFor flat key signatures, find the **second-last flat** reading from left to right. That note is your key.\n\n- **B-flat major** has two flats: B-flat and E-flat. The second-last flat is B-flat. So you're in B-flat major.\n- **E-flat major** has three flats: B-flat, E-flat, A-flat. The second-last flat is E-flat. So you're in E-flat major.\n\n> The key of **F major** is its own special case — it only has one flat (B-flat), so the flat trick doesn't apply. Just memorise it.\n\n### Make Sure Your Key Signature Is Visible\n\nWhen you hand your music to an accompanist, make sure the key signature is clearly visible and not obscured by annotations, cuts, or tape. This is fundamental information the pianist needs at a glance.",
        adminNotes: "Explain what key and time signatures are in plain, accessible language. Keep it practical and audition-relevant.",
